> For any topology $\T$ on $N$ define $p^*\T$ the topology of $M$ generated by $\T_M$ and the
> $\T_M$-connected components of $p^{-1}(\O)$ for $\O\in \T$. (l.1984)
>
> Let $\T$ be a (strong) thinner first countable and locally path connected topology on $N$ for
> which elements of $\Gamma(N/M)$ are continuous. […] Assume in addition there exists some
> $\R \subset \ord_p(N)$ strongly portly in $N$ on which strong and weak topologies agree. Then
> (a) $\widetilde M^S$ is strongly first countable connected locally path connected; (b) strong
> and weak topologies agree on $p^{-1}(\R)$; (c) $p^{-1}(\R)$ is strongly portly; (d) elements of
> $\Gamma(\widetilde M^S/M)$ are strongly continuous; (e) $\widetilde M^S\xrightarrow{p}N$ is a
> branched covering for the strong topologies. (l.1990–2004)
>
> Intuitively, if $(M,\T_M)\xrightarrow{p}(N,\T_N)$ is a spread and $\T$ is thinner than $\T_N$,
> then the strong topology $p^*\T$ is the coarsest topology on $M$ such that
> $(M,p^*\T)\xrightarrow{p}(N,\T)$ is a spread. However, the definition does not garantee that
> $\T_M$-connected components are $p^*\T$-connected components. Point $(e)$ above is thus
> non-trivial. (l.2006)

## Hypothesis checklist

| paper hypothesis | here |
|---|---|
| `lem:spread_top`: `M` T2, first countable, connected, LPC; `S` skeletal; `M ∖ S` slsc in `M` | ✓ carried (`hrel`); ⚠ strengthened: `M ∖ S` semilocally simply connected (`hSc`, under which the library builds `\widetilde M^S`, as in `GX.SpreadTopPrinted`) |
| sub-covering `\widetilde M^S → N → M` | ✓ read: `p`, `q` branched coverings with `q ∘ p` the projection (as in `GX.SpreadTopPrinted`); ✓ carried: `N` T2, LPC, first countable (DEC-66(a), the standing hypotheses) |
| `T` thinner, first countable, LPC, `Γ(N/M)` `T`-continuous; `R ⊆ ord_p N` `T`-portly, agreement on `R` | ✓ carried |
| (none) | ⚠ added (DEC-27), for (a)-connected and (e) only: `p⁻¹(R)` connected (`hRc`; the proof's "`R̂` weakly connected", l.2047) — without it (a) and (e) fail, even with `N → M` Galoisian and the local hypothesis (`GX.not_spreadTopGalLocalCE`, `GX.strongLocallyConnectedOrd_halfCut`) |
| (c), (e): (none) | ⚠ added: `GX.StrongLocallyConnectedOrd` (the Claim's "we may assume `Ô ∩ R̂` connected", l.2029), equivalent to (c); without it (c) and (e) fail even with `hRc` and `N → M` Galoisian (`GX.not_spreadTopGalConnCE`) |
| (d): (none) | ⚠ added: `N → M` Galoisian (`GX.spread_top_deck`, Models; DEC-71's Galoisian reading); necessity not formalized (informal: a non-normal intermediate covering) |
| `rem:quotient_virtually_tame`: `N → M` branched covering possibly branched over `sing M` | ✓ read: `Pj : \widetilde M → N` onto, over `M` (the universal property); ⚠ added: `\widetilde M → M` Galoisian; `D` injective, continuous for the quotient of `T`, `D ∘ Pj` equivariant through its holonomy `ρ` (data) |

## SCOPE (disclosed)

* **(a)** `lem:spread_top` is false as printed. In `GX.cex_spread_top_halfCut` (`M = N = ℂ`,
  `S = ∅`, `q = id`, `R = {Im ≠ 0}`, `T` the plane cut along the real axis, the axis glued to the
  upper half-plane) every hypothesis holds and `p^*T` is disconnected: (a) and (e) fail. The proof
  uses "`N` strongly connected" (l.2018) and "`R̂` weakly connected" (l.2047), neither a hypothesis.
  Even with `T` connected, `R̂` need not be connected (finding, not formalized: `ℂ^*`, `T` cutting
  along a ray glued to one bank, `\widetilde M^∅` the universal covering, `p^*T` falls into
  strips); the formal correction only needs `GX.cex_spread_top_halfCut`.
* **(b)** The Claim's last sentence "We may assume that `Ô ∩ R̂` is connected" (l.2029) is not
  proved, and the paper's argument for it (l.2034) lifts a path from `x̂` without ending at `ẑ`. It
  is exactly (c) (`GX.isPortly_strongPullTop_iff_local`), and it can fail: `GX.cex_spread_top_sector`
  (`M = N = ℂ`, `S = {0}`, `\widetilde M^S ≅ 𝔻_∞` the logarithmic covering completed at `0`,
  `GX.exists_homeomorph_maxBranched_qDisc`; `q = id`, `R = ℂ ∖ {0}`, portly for both topologies;
  `T = GX.sectorTop`, whose neighbourhoods of `0` are `{0} ∪ (disc ∩ {Re > 0})`). Every
  hypothesis holds, `hRc` included, and the preimage of the sector is `p^*T`-open and connected
  while its regular part falls into infinitely many sheets: (c), (e) and the local hypothesis fail.
* **(c)** `rem:hypothesis_model_spaces_necessary`: the massive particle `E_θ^{1,2}`, its maximal
  covering and the BTZ tamifying topology are not in the library (the latter is §4's
  `prop:BTZ_virt_tame`, l.2252, a forward dependency); the remark's assertions that the mixed
  topology `T'` is tamifying are not formalized. Proved: `T'` lies between `T` and the natural
  topology, is `T` on `V` and natural off `V`, is preserved by `T`-continuous homeomorphisms
  preserving `V`, and not by one moving a point off `V` to a point of `V` where `T` is strictly
  finer, with the toy instance `GX.hypothesis_model_spaces_toy` (`ℝ`, `cutTopReal`, a
  translation). The paper's "`V ⊂ {t>0}` or `V ⊂ {t>0}`" (l.2148) reads `{t<0}` second.
* **(d)** `rem:quotient_virtually_tame`: proved as an isomorphism of topological spaces (a quotient
  map with the orbits of the descended `Γ(\widetilde M/M)` as fibres), not of singular
  `(G,X)`-manifolds. The added hypothesis "`\widetilde M → M` Galoisian" is used (it makes the
  fibres of `N → M` orbits); it holds when `reg M` is semi-locally simply connected in `M`
  (`GX.maxBranched_isGaloisian_cc`). No counterexample to the remark without it is known: a
  narrowing, not a refutation. Injectivity of `D` makes every `γ` descend to `N`, so `N → M` is
  then Galoisian whatever `N` (`GX.descend_mem_deckMapsOf`).
* **(e)** `lem:spread_top` (d) is proved under `N → M` Galoisian (the proof's `γ̄`, l.2015, needs
  `γ` to descend to `N`). That (d) fails without it is NOT formalized (informal: a non-normal
  intermediate covering `N`, with `Γ(N/M)` trivial, carries a `T` that is cut at one point of a
  fibre and natural at the others, and a deck transformation of `\widetilde M^S` exchanging two
  sheets over them is not `p^*T`-continuous). The proof's reference "l.2068–2136" in the
  dispatch is the proof of `theo:local_virt_tame`; the proof of `lem:spread_top` is l.2010–2061.

